["Which is generally a result of higher biodiversity in an ecosystem?","greater resilience, because more species can fill roles if some are lost","lower productivity because species always compete","no change in stability","Higher biodiversity often helps an ecosystem recover from disruptions, since other species can perform roles of those lost, though effects vary."],
["Which list matches major human causes of biodiversity loss?","habitat destruction, invasive species, pollution, human population growth and overharvesting","volcanic eruptions, earthquakes, floods, fires and droughts","habitat restoration, native planting, recycling, conservation and regulated harvest","Habitat destruction, invasive species, pollution, population growth and overharvesting are widely cited human drivers; the other lists are natural events or helpful actions."],
["Using D = 1 − Σ(n/N)², what does a Simpson's diversity index close to 0 indicate about a community?","one or very few species dominate, so diversity is low","all species are equally abundant","the community has no individuals at all","The sum of squared proportions is near 1 when one species dominates, so D is near 0; a community with many evenly abundant species gives a D closer to 1."],
["Which statement about invasive species and biodiversity is best supported?","an invasive species can outcompete or prey on native species and reduce native biodiversity","invasive species always increase biodiversity because they add one more species","invasive species never affect the species around them","Invasive species can raise the species count briefly yet still harm native diversity by competition, predation, disease or habitat change."]
